version: "2.0"
description: >-
  Gnomon — the Paradigm 8.0 kernel (docs/specs/gnomon.md). One daemon per user
  owning sessions, identity, the hash-chained log, approvals, the budget fuse
  and the localhost web endpoint; bins gnomon, gnomond and gnomon-web. PR-0
  SCAFFOLD (research 29 §1/§4): the §18 layout with a src/ prefix, placeholder
  bins that print their version, the import fence, the zero-deps check, the
  G-WIN-05 registry skeleton and the M1.0 probe harness. Node >= 24 for Gnomon
  packages only; private and outside the root build/test scripts until the 8.0
  cut (G-MIG-01) — use `npm run build:gnomon` / `npm run test:gnomon`. Will ship
  later as a signed Node SEA (tsup emits one self-contained file per entry).

components:
  gnomon-kernel:
    description: >-
      The kernel package. ZERO runtime deps (G-SEC-01): package.json has no
      `dependencies` key; @a-company/gnomon-contracts is a devDependency that
      tsup bundles. Import fence (G-KRN-02): every import under src/kernel/**
      is node:*, relative, or @a-company/gnomon-contracts, and dist/ holds no
      bare import. src/kernel/ has the thirteen §18 modules (identity, log,
      runner, supervisor, broker, budget, scheduler, manifest, refs, socket,
      web, sandbox, modhost) as empty directories at PR-0. Placeholder bins
      write to stdio directly: the kernel cannot depend on the Paradigm logger
      (zero deps); #kernel-log brings its own logging at M1.2. PR-5 adds
      src/kernel/lifecycle/ — the $daemon-lifecycle core (spec §2.4): home
      resolution (~/.gnomon; GNOMON_HOME is a test seam only, a detached daemon
      gets --home in argv) created 0700 with the mode read back; the endpoint
      path (<home>/gnomon.sock, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR fallback on Linux on sun_path
      overflow, never /tmp, refuse if nothing fits; win32 refuses pending the
      P8-D78 parity phase); the O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile lock lifted from
      warpline lifecycle.ts (live holder refuses, dead holder cleaned by
      rename-and-inode-check and re-raced, empty file = racer mid-write); the
      clean re-exec env (PATH fixed pending root-signed config, HOME/USERPROFILE,
      LANG, LC_*; NODE_OPTIONS and GNOMON_* dropped); the idle-exit monitor
      (holders = open #socket connections until sessions/subscribers exist).
      bins: `gnomond [--foreground] [--home] [--idle-ms]`, `gnomon daemon
      status|stop|--detach` (stop refuses not-implemented pending
      #approval-broker, M1.6). Lifecycle events are emitted to a callback using
      the spec §9.1 kinds daemon.start, daemon.exit{reason}, daemon.lock.refused
      (the kernel log takes them at M1.2); the spec declares no signals.
      SYMBOL CONVENTION (P8-D81, test/symbols-drift.test.ts): every source
      module names the symbol(s) it implements in its header (first 40 lines),
      one `@symbol <id>` per line, using the spec Appendix A id verbatim where
      the spec has one (e.g. `@symbol #kernel-log`, `@symbol $daemon-lifecycle`).
      Every .ts under src/kernel/** must carry a tag; every tag must be
      declared in a packages/gnomon*/.purpose; every declared symbol must be an
      Appendix A node or be listed in test/symbol-allowlist.json with a reason.
      G-WIN-05 MARKER ENFORCEMENT (T-2026-10-17-011): vitest.config.ts excludes
      every test file whose `// @gnomon-platform:` marker omits the running
      process.platform (test/platform-registry.ts excludedTestFiles); each such
      file has a registry row (sibling or n/a reason), and the registry test
      proves per platform that the excluded set equals the rows that omit it,
      and that each row's platforms equal its file's marker.
    path: src/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, paradigm-8, scaffold]
    components:
      - "#gnomon-probes"
      - "#socket"
      - "#kernel-log"
      - "#identity"
      - "#ref-resolver"
      - "#web-endpoint"
      - "#oracle-sandbox"
      - "#manifest-loader"
      - "#scheduler"
      - "#session-runner"
      - "#supervisor"
      - "#claude-adapter"
    tests:
      - test/symbols-drift.test.ts
      - test/fence.test.ts
      - test/sec-01.test.ts
      - test/platform-registry.test.ts
      - test/version.test.ts
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/home-resolution.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
    oracles: [G-KRN-02, G-SEC-01, G-WIN-05, G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02]

  gnomon-probes:
    description: >-
      M1.0 probe harness (spec §19.1, research 29 §2). `node
      packages/gnomon/probes/run.mjs <id> [--out-dir d] [--timeout ms]` imports
      probes/<id>/probe.mjs (zero deps, no build), runs its default export with
      {id, platform, arch, probeDir, signal}, and writes a probeResult:v1 record
      to probes/results/<id>.<platform>.json. Throws and timeouts become verdict
      `fail`. Exit codes 0 pass / 1 fail / 2 partial / 64 usage. Probe ids match
      ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$ (no path traversal). p-harness is the self-test and
      its results are gitignored; real results are committed from hardware runs
      only. A `fail` on a ruling means a re-plan memo before dependent builds.
      Unsigned macOS probes: p10m-sbx-deny-mac (S29-03/04 sandbox-exec session
      denies + escape matrix), p11-ws-framing (RFC 6455 over node:http with a
      forked server child, 27 Autobahn-style raw cases, 4 MiB throughput, RSS
      and phys_footprint), p-noise-vectors (Noise IK/XX/XXpsk3 over node:crypto
      against a verbatim cacophony/snow vector subset in vectors.json, G-LNK-11)
      p-log-fsync (kernel-log head-anchor fsync stalls, arms A-G; arm G =
      the research 33 policy: coalesced anchor + forced fullfsync+anchor at
      1/64, judged by the restated G-LOG-04; env P_LOG_FSYNC_ARMS accepts
      A,E,G) and p01-claude-cli (read-only `claude --version`/`--help` flag check;
      live items pending-manual). M1.0 batch 2 (probes/README.md lists them):
      unattended p06-local-caps (llama.cpp /slots n_ctx + live tool-call
      detection; no endpoint = partial, recorded not failed), p09-ed25519-matrix
      (WebCrypto Ed25519 in Node + every local engine under the §11.2 CSP;
      safari.html for Safari by hand, P09_SAFARI_LINE to record it),
      p12-excalidraw-csp (pinned Excalidraw bundled by esbuild in a temp dir,
      loaded under the §11.2 CSP; violations by phase, size, parse time),
      p14-link-outbound (default-deny sandbox-exec link profile); founder-run
      p00-sign-notary, p04-se-helper, p05-keychain-acl (signed
      GnomonHelperProbe.app from _shared/helper/ProbeHelper.swift; fail fast
      with a founder-steps pointer when the identity / provisioning profile /
      gnomon-notary profile is missing; prompt-free halves run unattended),
      p07-launch-token (NSWorkspace hand-off, eslogger + log stream + ps
      capture with an argv positive control, browser history grep incl.
      mozlz4), p01-claude-cli-manual (hook session_id, control_request
      interrupt, loopback-override observation, managed policy; claude by the
      path p01 recorded, never PATH). s29-10-voice-custody is a README stub
      pending the signed helper. p14b-link-fdpass (research paradigm-8/37):
      the kernel stand-in dials the peer and passes the connected socket over
      IPC to a deny-default child with no network* allow; full duplex 4 MiB,
      peer close, redial hand-off, child connect/UDP/listen/unix/file/exec all
      EPERM. probes/_shared/ (browser.mjs, signing.mjs, abort.mjs — synchronous
      cleanups on SIGINT/SIGTERM/ctx.signal for founder-run probes, ask()
      rejects on abort — wkwebview-load.swift, helper/) is shared code, not a
      probe id. p12 pins its whole tree with a committed package-lock.json
      (npm ci). CI probe results go to $RUNNER_TEMP, never probes/results/.
    path: probes/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, probes, m1.0]
    tests:
      - test/probe-harness.test.ts

  socket:
    description: >-
      #socket (spec §2.1, §4.9, §5) — the kernel's gnomon-rpc:v1 endpoint.
      PR-5 core: unix-socket server bound under a 0177 umask, chmod 0600 and
      the ACL read back before serving; byte-based NDJSON framing (4 MiB max
      inbound frame, UTF-8 safe partial lines, oversize discarded to its
      newline and answered BAD_REQUEST once, malformed lines BAD_REQUEST,
      connection kept); per-connection in-order replies; connIds are
      conn:<ULID>. M1.4 SLICE 1: errors carry contracts refusal:v1 in
      error.data with the §4.9 code map (rpc.ts). auth.ts — `hello`
      negotiation via contracts negotiateHello (highest shared gnomon-rpc
      version; none shared ⇒ UNSUPPORTED{offered[], supported[]}; tolerant
      param validation; client strings capped at 128 chars) answering
      {clientApi, features, nonce, serverKeyId, serverSig, clientApiId,
      kitId}; serverSig = kernel key over gnomon:auth-server:v1 + nonce +
      endpointPath; `auth {principal, deviceId, sig}` verified against the
      ROOT-signed device registry (#identity), nonce single-use (any attempt
      consumes it) and 30 s, accepted transcripts remembered so a replay is
      socket.auth.replay; {token}/{launchToken}/{reconnect} refused
      UNSUPPORTED (later M1.4 slices). Any failed auth = ONE AUTH refusal,
      then the connection is closed. dispatch.ts — before auth only hello and
      auth (other known methods AUTH{unauthenticated}, connection kept;
      unknown names UNKNOWN_VERB); after auth the §5 verb × principal matrix
      (contracts VERBS.access; peer refused everywhere) then `status` served
      for real (principal, connId, surface, clientApi, clientApiId, kitId,
      abiId, nextLegalVerbs from the §5 table, verbs/relays with
      availability + milestone, daemon info, log head, sessions 0, cards 0,
      kernelKeyCustody partial, root state, authenticated clients) under
      50 ms (G-ABI-03), every other verb/relay ENGINE{not-implemented,
      milestone} — no fake success, no extra verb (the PR-5 daemon.status /
      daemon.stop placeholders are removed). Kernel-log rows before the
      reply: socket.hello, socket.auth.ok, socket.auth.fail,
      socket.auth.replay (+ socket.connect, socket.frame.refused as
      diagnostics); a failed auth.ok write refuses the auth. client.ts —
      rpcCall, RpcConnection, verifyServerHello (serverSig against the
      registry's kernel key over the CLIENT-derived path), signAuth and
      connectGnomon (never sends auth to an unverified server; reason
      unverified-server). ROUND 2: both signatures also bind the negotiated
      clientApi + canonical features (downgrade-proofing; a deviation from the
      §4.9 preimage text, pending spec/contracts); the registry is cached by
      size+mtime+sha256; qualified matrix access (e.g. mod 'own view') is
      refused ENGINE not-implemented with its milestone, never the full view;
      hello/auth.ok log failures close the connection (fail rows are
      best-effort); pre-auth limits — no hello in 10 s or no auth by the 30 s
      nonce TTL closes, at most 64 pre-auth connections; flow control pauses
      reading at 32 queued requests or 1 MiB unsent replies; pre-auth
      diagnostics are coalesced into one signed counter row per window.
      ROUND 3: socket.hello and socket.auth.fail are rate-limited — the
      first 8 per 10 s window individually, the rest folded into that
      window's signed counter row (byOutcome / byReason); auth.ok/.replay stay
      individual; inbound frames capped at 16 KiB until auth succeeds; the
      client refuses a hello whose clientApi/features it did not offer
      (hello-not-offered, client-local); RegistryCache parses the bytes it
      hashed. Windows named pipe: parity phase (P8-D78).
      M1.4 SLICE 2 (log-read.ts): `log.read` and `ref.resolve` served for
      authenticated principals, each handled EXPLICITLY per reader class
      (never the generic 'full'): human → the §9.3 COMMUNITY-CLIENT projection
      (full minus sensitive and secret, sensitive-column kinds as {kind, hsid,
      at, hash} stubs, registered sensitive fields → {redacted, hash}) because
      a first-party surface / log.read:sensitive grant cannot be proven on
      the socket yet (result names projection + fullView.needs); mod → own
      modns.<name>.* + granted kinds (none yet); agent → UNSUPPORTED Move 2;
      voice → UNSUPPORTED Move 4; session → ENGINE not-implemented M1.7 (no
      principal → hsid binding); read tokens do not exist yet. Pages:
      {since, until?, kinds? (exact or ns.*), scope? {hsid, repo, loop,
      contract} applied to the PROJECTED view (a stub never matches a
      repo/loop/contract filter), limit? ≤ 1000} → {rows, next (last scanned
      seq, stable cursor), head, caughtUp, projection, withheld}; ≤ 10 000
      rows scanned and ~2 MiB per page, a row > 1 MiB becomes {view:
      'oversize', kind, hash, bytes}. Subscriptions (follow: true): reply
      {subscription, since, head, projection, backlogCap} written first
      (ctx.afterReply), then backlog + live rows as `event` notifications
      {subscription, row} in seq order, read lazily from the log; the pump
      writes only under the connection's write high-water mark and resumes on
      'drain'; per subscription a queue of undelivered post-subscribe seqs
      (numbers only) — beyond 10 000 the subscription is dropped, the client
      gets event{subscription, end{reason:'dropped', lag, since}} and the
      kernel logs socket.subscription.dropped{subscription, lag, since} with
      the connId (deferred, never inside a commit); ≤ 8 subscriptions per
      connection, 256 per kernel; each holds the idle monitor ('subscriber');
      subscriptions end with their connection. server.ts gains ctx.notify /
      writableLength / writeHighWater / onDrain / afterReply; client.ts
      RpcConnection.onNotification. status adds subscriptions{n,
      onThisConnection, perConnectionMax, backlogCap}. ROUND 2: limited
      views (community-client, mod, read-token) carry no hash/sig/prev,
      redacted fields are {redacted: true} and stubs {kind, hsid?, at} (no
      confirmation oracle; ref.resolve returns no hash to them); only rows at
      or below the log's RELEASED head are read or streamed (failed critical
      rows never); lag = committed head − cursor, a subscribe with head −
      since > 10 000 is BAD_REQUEST{backlog-too-large} (page first); the
      commit-path fan-out is one metadata check per subscriber (mayBeVisible
      + kind filter; no projection, copy or hash — projection happens lazily
      in the pump); writeHighWater is clamped to ≥ the socket's
      writableHighWaterMark; an unencodable frame ends the subscription with
      end{reason:'internal'}; follow on a JSON-RPC notification (no id) is
      refused follow-needs-id; queued requests of a closed connection never
      run (server `closed` flag) and dispatch refuses ENGINE{gone} for a
      closed ctx, so zombie pre-auth entries cannot fill maxPreAuth. ROUND
      3: limited views also drop idem and refHash; lag = min(seq, released
      head) − cursor; subscribe admits at most cap/2 backlog measured
      against the released head (page until caughtUp, then follow).
      M1.4 SLICE 3 (round 2: P8-D89 / D89a): read tokens are PROOF OF
      POSSESSION — auth {tokenId, sig} (exactly those keys, after hello, inside
      the nonce TTL), sig = the token's Ed25519 key over gnomon:auth-token:v1 +
      contracts authTokenMessage(nonce, serverKeyId, tokenId, endpointPath,
      negotiated hello), verified against the public key in #identity's
      root-signed token file; a bearer {token} is auth-params; the seed never
      crosses the wire. The connection binds to the token's principal with
      credential 'read-token' and matrix class readToken: status, log.read
      (the §9.3 read-token projection), ref.resolve; every other verb and both
      relays FORBIDDEN{matrix}. Unknown / expired / not-yet-valid / revoked
      (sticky) / bad or replayed proof / in use (one live connection per
      token) / untrusted token file ⇒ AUTH + close; the token is re-checked on
      every call, at its expiry and every 5 s; a dead token closes the
      connection with a best-effort socket.disconnect{reason, tokenId}. A
      connection that closes during the awaited auth.ok write is refused
      ENGINE{gone} and gets no timers or lifted cap (timerCount() for tests).
      Rows carry only tokenId. connectGnomon({readToken}) validates options
      before connecting and proves possession only to a verified server.
      G-SOCK-01: a known verb before auth is AUTH and CLOSES the connection.
      log-append.ts — the §5 namespace-ownership gate (G-SOCK-04): unknown and
      kernel-owned kinds FORBIDDEN{kernel-owned} for every class, the human
      request.* exception too (needs: first-party human-class connection,
      unprovable until #web-endpoint); voice request.voice.arm and voice.*
      ENGINE Move 4; userland kinds FORBIDDEN{ungranted, M1.5 — grant.bind
      needs M1.3 grants over M1.5 manifests} for human/agent; a mod's own
      modns ENGINE M1.11, else modns-not-own; nothing is ever appended in M1.4.
      status adds credential, token{tokenId, expiresAt}, relay access, and
      nextLegalVerbs filtered to the caller's class. Preimages come from
      contracts (T-2026-10-17-017). abiId pinned in test/abi.ids.json
      (G-ABI-04). Suites: test/socket-abi.test.ts (G-ABI-01..05 incl. a literal
      §5 matrix fixture and the userland-namespace list, G-SOCK-02..04, the
      auth race; todos naming later milestones). ROUND 3: the PoP is verified
      before any token state is told (no valid proof ⇒ uniform unknown-token,
      no tokenId on the row); the daemon re-reads the token file at start and
      every 60 s (tokenRefreshMs) so revocations reach the chain with no live
      token connection, retrying a failed key.token.revoked write and keeping
      it pending (daemon.token.revocation.unrecorded event) — never dropped;
      every reason the kernel sends is registered (test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
      scans socket/, web/, refs/, redaction.ts). M1.4 #web-endpoint: auth.ts
      takes a `web` hook for {launchToken}/{reconnect} (credential 'gui',
      human class); on the unix socket those forms stay UNSUPPORTED
      web-auth-not-implemented.
    path: src/kernel/socket/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, socket, paradigm-8, pr-5]
    tests:
      - test/ndjson-framing.test.ts
      - test/socket-server.test.ts
      - test/socket-auth.test.ts
      - test/socket-log-read.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
    aspects: ["~ample-internal-logs"]
    oracles: [G-DMN-02, G-ABI-01, G-ABI-02, G-ABI-03, G-ABI-04, G-ABI-05, G-SOCK-01, G-SOCK-02, G-SOCK-03, G-SOCK-04]

  web-endpoint:
    description: >-
      #web-endpoint (spec §2.1, §4.9 WebSocket paragraph, §11.2, §15.5
      `transport`, §2.4 linger; M1.4). The kernel half (src/kernel/web/) and
      the `gnomon-web` transport child (src/transport/). The CHILD parses
      bytes only: node:http on the 127.0.0.1 literal (ephemeral port), the
      RFC 6455 upgrade and zero-dep framing (ws-codec.ts: text only — binary
      1003; no fragmentation — FIN=0/continuation 1009; no extension ever
      negotiated — RSV bits 1002; masking required 1002; invalid UTF-8 1007;
      >4 MiB 1009 from the header; close mirrored; pings every 30 s, closes
      after 2 missed pongs), and static serving of the bundle bytes it hashed
      at start (held in memory). It forwards {rid, method, path, headers} /
      {rid, data} records over stdio (protocol.ts) and writes back only what
      the kernel says; its import closure is framing + channel + bundle hash
      (test/seams-fence.test.ts). The KERNEL decides every rule (rules.ts):
      Host = 127.0.0.1:<port> else 421 (host) before routing; Origin =
      http://127.0.0.1:<port> on every upgrade and non-static request else
      403 (origin); Sec-Fetch-Site same-origin|none else 403; no cookies, no
      ACAO, the rule-6 CSP/COOP/CORP/nosniff/no-referrer on every response,
      no-store on non-static; traversal refused; /blob raster-only by content
      sniffing and verified content hash, GUI token in X-Gnomon-Token; /diag
      ≤ 64 KiB to <home>/logs/gui.log after redactDiagText (32-byte
      base64url tokens ⟨redacted⟩, contracts display sanitizer, Aegis C5); /blob and /diag are POST (a
      same-origin GET carries no Origin; the page sends them with
      referrerPolicy same-origin), GET/HEAD 405; /rpc and /diag match
      exactly, only /blob/ is a prefix; no HTTP RPC. Every child record is
      validated against the closed shape set (protocol.ts
      validateChildRecord) and the child is SIGKILLed on the first violation
      or on any throw in record handling (Aegis C1); a compromised child is a
      compromise of the GUI sessions it relays (spec §A.7). Child-written
      503/404 carry the rule-6 default headers. Per connection: the child
      starts at a 16 KiB inbound cap, raised by a kernel `limit` record on
      authentication; unsent bytes are reported as pressure/drain (the
      dispatcher's writableLength/onDrain are per connection) and over 8 MiB
      the child closes 1008; more than 32 queued requests close 1008. Read
      tokens do not authenticate on the web endpoint (Aegis C3). WebSocket: hello then
      auth within 5 s else 1008, frames before auth never dispatched as verbs;
      ≤ 64 live (non-closing) connections, at the cap the oldest live
      unauthenticated one is closed (1013), never an authenticated GUI one
      (503 instead); ≤ 5 auth
      attempts/min/connection. Its own dispatcher (authenticator signs
      ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/rpc) with a `web` auth hook (launch.ts): a
      single-use 60 s launch token minted in-process for an authenticated
      human device connection (DaemonHandle.requestWebLaunch — §5 names no
      verb for `gnomon open`), exchanged for a 32-byte GUI token held as a
      digest; a second presentation inside the TTL is theft (refused, the
      consuming session revoked, socket.auth.theft + socket.gui.session.ended
      {theft}); after the TTL a replay (socket.auth.replay only); both answer
      `replayed-transcript`. Reconnect logs socket.auth.reconnect.requested
      and is refused UNSUPPORTED web-auth-not-implemented {milestone M1.3,
      cause presence-unavailable} until the signed helper's presence. GUI
      sessions live as long as their socket (≤ 12 h; revoke-all; daemon
      exit). Bundle pin (bundle.ts): gnomon-bundle-tree:v1 hash; since
      M1.10 slice 1 the #gui bundle (packages/gnomon-gui, embedded as opaque
      base64 in the GENERATED gui-bundle.ts, read by gui-pin.ts outside the child closure) pinned by the GUI_BUNDLE_HASH
      literal until the root-signed `gnomon gui pin` (M1.3), materialized
      under <home>/gui/bundle/ (the placeholder bundle stays a fixture);
      a mismatch logs
      socket.web.bundle.mismatch and never opens. Supervision: restart cap
      3/10 min (socket.web.transport.exited, then socket.web.stop
      {transport-restart-cap}; a failed respawn ⇒ {transport-restart-failed};
      both clear the timers); 2-min linger after the last GUI session
      (socket.web.linger, socket.web.stop{no-sessions}). Production spawn
      only through the platform's `transport` sandbox profile
      (#oracle-sandbox; interim residual T-2026-10-18-017: on macOS that
      profile lets the child listen on every configured interface, LAN
      included, until the kernel-accept handoff P14b-web
      T-2026-10-18-020 lands); the unsandboxed child exists only via the internal
      seam `web` (testing.ts createWebEndpointForTest). Event kinds emitted
      (§9.1 socket namespace): socket.web.listen, socket.web.bundle.mismatch,
      socket.web.linger, socket.web.stop, socket.web.transport.exited,
      socket.gui.token.issued, socket.gui.session.ended, socket.auth.theft,
      socket.auth.replay, socket.auth.reconnect.requested, socket.auth.fail
      {origin|host|sec-fetch-site|gui-token}. The spec declares no signals.
    path: src/kernel/web/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, web-endpoint, transport, paradigm-8, m1-4]
    tests:
      - test/web-units.test.ts
      - test/web-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/gui-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/web-child.test.ts
      - test/web-sandbox.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
      - test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
    oracles: [G-WEB-01, G-WEB-02, G-WEB-03, G-WEB-04, G-WEB-05, G-WEB-06, G-WEB-07, G-WEB-08]

  oracle-sandbox:
    description: >-
      #oracle-sandbox (spec §15.5) — the kernel's sandbox profiles. M1.4
      lands the `transport` profile for gnomon-web (src/kernel/sandbox/
      transport.ts): profile data, the darwin sandbox-exec SBPL generator
      (default-deny; read of `/`, /usr/lib, /System/Library, the node binary
      and its install's lib/ — never the prefix — plus Homebrew's Cellar, opt
      and etc/openssl@3; refused `sandbox-unavailable` if any granted read
      subpath equals or contains $HOME, the gnomon home or its keys dir
      (Aegis C2);
      the child's entry dir and package.json ancestors, the pinned bundle;
      metadata of their ancestors; sysctl-read; loopback bind/inbound; exec
      of node only; no fork, no write, no mach-lookup, no outbound; every
      path realpath'd; env empty). Platform registry TRANSPORT_BACKENDS
      (G-WIN-05): darwin only; win32 is the Windows parity phase (P8-D78);
      any platform without a backend refuses `sandbox-unavailable` (fail
      closed). Residuals: SBPL `localhost:*` also admits ::1 (the child binds
      127.0.0.1 only); ancestor metadata is visible; node may re-exec itself.
      T-2026-10-18-017 (measured, for Aegis): SBPL accepts only `*` or
      `localhost` as a network host, and `(local ip "localhost:*")` matches
      every address configured on this host's interfaces — the LAN address
      and the 0.0.0.0 / :: wildcards included, an unconfigured 127.0.0.2
      denied — so the profile is NOT loopback-only (the child's own code binds
      127.0.0.1; a compromised child could listen on the LAN and be used as a
      remote channel to the GUI sessions it relays). Not expressible in SBPL;
      fd-passing does not help (listen() on an inherited fd is re-checked by
      network-inbound). Profile unchanged; spec §A.7 and the §15.5 transport
      row record it as an interim residual. Revisit trigger: the
      kernel-accept handoff (P14b-web, T-2026-10-18-020), required before
      Move 1 exit.
      The one-off macos-14 `listen EPERM 127.0.0.1` was not reproduced (0 of
      170 runs); web-sandbox.test.ts retries ONLY the loopback-listen leg (≤3,
      each failure annotated), never the denials, never in the kernel.
      The other seven profiles land with their milestones (session M1.7,
      oracle M2.3, …).
    path: src/kernel/sandbox/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, sandbox, paradigm-8, m1-4]
    tests:
      - test/web-sandbox.test.ts
      - test/web-units.test.ts
    oracles: [G-WEB-07]

  ref-resolver:
    description: >-
      #ref-resolver (spec §3.7, §4.4, §5 verb 12; M1.4 slice 2) —
      src/kernel/refs/resolve.ts, the kernel's read-only resolver for its
      built-in schemes. Served: `ev:<ULID>` → {ref, target: the §9.3
      projected view for the caller's reader class, hash}; every row is
      re-hashed (mismatch ⇒ ENGINE{row-integrity}, withheld); a missing row
      and an invisible one are the same NOT_FOUND (no existence oracle); an
      @rev on an event is BAD_REQUEST. Validated then ENGINE{not-implemented,
      milestone}: turn:<hsid>/<n> and hsid: (M1.7), cc (M1.7), gkey/dev
      (M1.3), permit/card (M1.6), contract/node (M2.1), pev (Move 2 — the
      spec names none). UNSUPPORTED: peer: (only a brief's copy, §3.9),
      package schemes (no-resolver: userland), unknown schemes. Malformed
      refs, dropped schemes (voice) and refs over 512 chars are BAD_REQUEST.
    path: src/kernel/refs/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, refs, paradigm-8, m1.4]
    tests:
      - test/socket-log-read.test.ts
    oracles: [G-ABI-02]

  identity:
    description: >-
      #identity (spec §3.1; M1.4 slice of it — hardware custody is M1.3,
      blocked on the founder's provisioning profile). root-authority.ts — the
      RootAuthority seam: UnprovisionedRootAuthority is the production default
      (verifies nothing, signRow refuses UNSUPPORTED{root-unprovisioned,
      milestone M1.3}; never a software fallback); DevTestRootAuthority is a
      software P-256 root constructible only with the literal
      DEV_TEST_ROOT_ACK, never from env/argv (§15.3), reported as root state
      `dev-test` in status and daemon.start; RootVerifier verifies rows from a
      pinned root public key (the client side). Row sigs: gnomon:row-sig:v1
      over canonical JSON, P-256 IEEE-P1363. device-key.ts — the Ed25519
      device key at <home>/keys/device.key: written O_EXCL 0600, loaded only
      if a regular owner-only file in an owner-only keys/ dir; a device
      signer cannot approve. registry.ts — <home>/keys/registry.jsonl,
      append-only {row, signer, sig} rows (kind kernel | device); fail-closed
      reader: untrusted signer, bad signature, keyId ≠ sha256(PEM), principal
      or deviceId outside the root's ns, foreign principals and peer/kernel
      kinds never resolve and are surfaced in rejected[]; first row pins a
      deviceId and a keyId. The kernel principal is p:<root ns>/kernel, or
      p:00000000/kernel while no root exists (UNPROVISIONED_NS).
      kernel-pub.ts — PUBLIC halves of every kernel key the home ran with
      (<home>/keys/kernel-pub.jsonl, 0600), loaded into the log's key ring and
      kernelKeyIds so verify works across restarts; same-user writable and not
      root-signed, so across restarts kernel signatures prove nothing against
      a same-user forger until M1.3 (custody partial). presence.ts
      (T-2026-10-17-009(c), Aegis S34-10) — PresenceToken, a branded type
      minted only by this module: private constructor guarded by a
      module-private mint key, a `#state` private field and a `unique symbol`
      brand (nominal by type; `PresenceToken.is` is a `#state in v` runtime
      brand check no copy, prototype object or Proxy passes); bound to one
      purpose (`log.gap.acknowledge`; M1.5 adds `mod.install` and
      `mod.rollback`), single-use and fresh for 60 s
      (`consumePresence` → ok | not-a-token | wrong-purpose | stale | used).
      `requestPresence` refuses UNSUPPORTED{presence-unavailable, M1.3} until
      the signed helper's prompt exists; the only mint before then is the
      internal test seam (method `test-seam`).
      read-token.ts (M1.4 slice 3, P8-D89a) — <home>/keys/read-tokens.jsonl,
      append-only root-signed rows {op: mint, tokenId rtk:<26>, keyId,
      publicKeyPem, principalId (root ns, not kernel, not foreign), createdAt,
      expiresAt (default 1 h, ≤ 24 h)} and {op: revoke, tokenId}; PUBLIC keys
      only. mintReadToken (library only: no verb, no argv; now within 5 min of
      the real clock and clamped to it, so expiresAt ≤ real now + ttl) returns grt:v1:<id>.<seed> once; parseReadToken gives
      the client a readToken-class signer that signs only gnomon:auth-token:v1.
      ReadTokenStore: fail-closed reader; not valid before createdAt − 60 s;
      revocations STICKY for its life and seeded from key.token.revoked rows
      on the chain (daemon.ts records each newly seen revoke); the file must
      grow append-only — shrinking or a lost/rewritten line, or more than
      1 MiB / 10 000 rows, makes it untrusted for the store's life (nothing
      resolves; key.registry.malformed{file, reason}). Open questions (spec
      silent; most restrictive reading chosen): the CLI verb that mints, the
      revocation re-check period.
    path: src/kernel/identity/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, identity, paradigm-8, m1.4]
    tests:
      - test/socket-auth.test.ts
      - test/presence.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts

  manifest-loader:
    description: >-
      #manifest-loader + policy (spec §3.6, §4.3, §12.5, §15.4, §15.5, §15.7,
      §16; §19.1 M1.5). src/kernel/manifest/. manifest.ts — manifest:v1
      validation, producer-strict and fail-closed (wire form: the JSON subset
      of the §4.3 YAML in gnomon-manifest.json, zero deps): unknown keys and
      trust:/source:/postinstall refused; kind: mod needs abi mod-abi:v1, an
      unknown abi refused; a name equal to a kernel namespace token refused;
      kind adapter/provider first-party only, a provider ships only
      provides.knowledge; tool effects ⊆ read|write|network|spend; card.* and
      status.mod slots append-only; events under modns.<name>.*; keybindings
      bind declared commands, never the reserved decision keys; capabilities
      are §15.4 forms (approve/band:write never; voice.approve:<class> never
      from a manifest; knowledge:federate/link:send first-party only;
      log.append only on its own modns); provides.knowledge needs
      knowledge:provide and never egress. manifestHash = sha256(canonical
      JSON); detectWidening = additions by field (effects, egress,
      capabilities, events, tools, views, commands, keybindings, knowledge).
      egress.ts — Anthropic hosts (anthropic.com, claude.ai, claude.com and
      subdomains, and any wildcard covering them) refused in manifests and
      grant rows (G-MAN-01). snapshot.ts — the snapshot copier: copies a
      package or linked folder to <home>/mods/<name>/pinned/<hex>/ (owner-only,
      read-only), packageHash over the sorted tree of the COPY (exact bytes,
      .git skipped unread), refusing symlink/junction, hardlink (nlink > 1),
      .node addon, a path escaping the folder, special files, case-colliding
      paths and a swap between walk and read (fstat dev/ino), emitting
      mod.snapshot.refused + manifest.refuse{SNAPSHOT}; lockfile rule
      (node_modules needs an npm lockfile, the card lists every
      package@version; other formats refused, LOCKFILE); lifecycle install
      scripts refused (POSTINSTALL); diffTrees for the re-approve card.
      grants.ts — <home>/grants.jsonl, root-signed rows through the
      RootAuthority seam (production refuses until M1.3), fail-closed reader
      (unsigned, untrusted signer, bad signature, unknown kind, malformed,
      Anthropic egress, egress beside knowledge:provide → rejected[]);
      grant.install carries `name` beside the §15.4 fields; the effective pin
      is the last grant.install row. install.ts — stageInstall (package or
      linked) raises a PENDING card:v1 kind install with its detail (name,
      source, tier, effects, egress, capabilities, events, tools, views, read
      paths, package@version list, widening, file diff, stagedBy) and signs
      nothing (manifest.staged). The kernel-composed line `runs commands: N
      mcp server(s), M hook(s)` follows the packageHash line, before any
      manifest-controlled line (Aegis round 2 C2); mcp.command, mcp.args,
      hooks.command and hooks.event refuse SCHEMA on C0/C1, Zl, Zp and Cf
      (U+2028/U+2029 would otherwise draw a fake card line, C1). The card's lines put every mcp server and
      every hook on its OWN line and are sanitized and WRAPPED by code points
      under the broker's MAX_LINE (continuations `  ↳ `), never cut, so a long
      mcp argument list cannot push a hook out of sight; the sanitizer flags
      travel with the lines (blockFor → rederiveInstallDetail → the broker),
      and contentHash still covers the full detail (T-2026-10-18-015 item 2);
      loadMod loads only from a pinned snapshot
      under a root-signed grant.install, re-hashes it (INTEGRITY), checks
      grants, and for a linked folder re-hashes the source: a change pauses
      the mod (mod.paused) and stages one re-approve card (manifest.widened
      when widened). The card DECISION and the human rollback (re-pin a
      previously approved snapshot, mod.rolled-back) are module-private and
      registered only on the internal seam channel (slot `manifest`), reached
      only through src/kernel/testing.ts (decideInstallCard,
      rollbackModSnapshot) until the M1.6 approval broker owns cards; both
      need a PresenceToken (mod.install / mod.rollback) and a signing root.
      layers.ts — layersHash (managed, user settings*.json + CLAUDE.md +
      skills/plugins/agents/commands listings, project and worktree .claude/**
      + CLAUDE.md + CLAUDE.local.md) and gitHash (.git/config, hooks/**,
      info/** in the common dir, a worktree's .git file followed,
      .gitattributes), CRLF→LF and case-folded NFC path keys on every platform
      (G-WIN-04). policy.ts — the per-session policy DATA for the `session`
      profile (keys/Warpline-token/browser read denies, ~/.gnomon + settings
      layers + linked folders write denies, endpoint-port loopback deny,
      process-info deny-others, launchers deny; realpath'd; policyHash) and the
      matching policy.json permissions block — the platform profile is
      generated by M1.7. generate.ts — plugin.json, .mcp.json, hooks.json and
      .gnomon/.gitignore from a kind: distro manifest into the repo's
      .gnomon/, each with a marker and a ledger; a fixed point; never
      overwrites a file that is not ours; uninstallRepo removes exactly the
      ledger's unedited files and the dirs it created (project.yaml stays);
      uninstallHome removes ~/.gnomon or returns it as the export path.
      ROUND 2 (review + Aegis F1–F11): generate.ts confines every path to the
      fixed generated set under <repo>/.gnomon (lstat each component, refuse
      links/junctions, realpath inside .gnomon, temp file O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW +
      rename) and keys ownership on the manifestHash marker plus the
      kernel-held record <home>/generated/<sha256(realpath repo)>.json (the
      repo holds no ledger); the session policy write-denies the whole
      ~/.claude/** and ~/.claude.json (both also hashed into layersHash — a
      gap in spec §3.3/§15.5 to amend) and .git/hooks|config|info (gitHash
      inputs; §15.5 amendment), and carries the platform-neutral deny classes
      (tmux/screen sockets, helper IPC, sysmond, AppleEvents, service
      manager, mic/camera, launchers) mapped per platform in
      src/platform/session-denies.ts; widening covers mcp and hooks and the
      card lists commands, keybindings, knowledge, mcp, hooks and opaque
      array counts; the lockfile rule is case-folded, applies at any depth
      and cross-checks shipped package.json versions; the tier is derived
      (linked ⇒ local, first-party only from a bundled packageHash allowlist,
      empty for now; a signed claim is refused until trusted keys exist);
      decideInstall validates the cardId, recomputes contentHash, re-derives
      the detail from the pinned snapshot, appends grant.install LAST and is
      idempotent; egress refuses ports, numeric hosts, single labels and
      local/private names; symlinked layers hash the target bytes and
      .claude/worktrees/ is excluded. The case-collision oracle is
      exercised on Linux CI (a case-insensitive FS cannot hold the pair).
      Events: §9.1 kinds only (manifest.*, mod.*); the spec declares no
      signals. Refusals are refusal:v1 with data.manifestCode (not
      data.reason, which would move clientApiId).
    path: src/kernel/manifest/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, manifest, policy, paradigm-8, m1.5]
    tests:
      - test/manifest-loader.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
    oracles: [G-MAN-01, G-MAN-02, G-MAN-03, G-HON-04, G-LAW-04, G-WIN-04]

  kernel-log:
    description: >-
      #kernel-log (spec §3.2, M1.2; research 29 §4 PR-4) — the one
      hash-chained, append-only, segmented kernel log. node:sqlite; active
      segment <home>/kernel.db, sealed segments <home>/log/segments/NNNNNN.db
      (0600, dirs 0700); `chain` table per §3.2 plus a write-once `meta`;
      BEFORE UPDATE/DELETE triggers abort (L1). hash = sha256(canonical(envelope
      minus hash, sig)), prev(1) = sha256("gnomon-log-genesis:" + logId), seq
      computed inside the write txn and checked against the in-memory tip.
      Envelope = contracts event:v1, validated strict with default-label check;
      unregistered kinds refused (never guessed), labels tighten-only (L6),
      kernel/userland ownership enforced (L7), daemon.segment reserved to the
      log, session.inject must carry {origin, hash, bytes}. Signer seam
      (signer.ts): Signer.sign(domain, message) prepends the §3.1 domain
      (gnomon:event-sig:v1 etc.), key classes limited to their domains;
      SoftwareEd25519Signer = the in-memory kernel key (keyId
      gkey:v1:<sha256(spki PEM)>); root/approvals keys are hardware-only and
      plug in after probes P04/P02; KeyRing verifies Ed25519 and P-256.
      Unsigned rows only for daemon/socket/schedule; the §3.2 mandatory list
      refuses unsigned (unsigned-mandatory). Segments rotate at 10⁶ rows with a
      signed daemon.segment closing row and an unbroken chain across files;
      crash mid-rotation is completed on open. Anchors: file <home>/head
      (atomic tmp+rename; fsync only at genesis/rotation/close) and a
      KeystoreAnchorStore SEAM that reports `unavailable` until the helper
      exists (AnchorStore.write may be async; keystore writes are serialised).
      ANCHOR POLICY (anchor-policy.ts; research 33 S33-05..07, P8-D82):
      `append` is async and resolves {seq, hash, anchored, event}. Bulk rows
      coalesce the file anchor (renamed when 63 rows are un-anchored or 50 ms
      after the first, unref'd timer + inline check, and on checkpoint) and
      queue the keystore anchor every 1023 rows / 1 s off the append path.
      CRITICAL_ANCHOR_KINDS (approval.decide/suspect/presence.denied/
      rule.learned/voice.arm/voice.disarm, key.*, grant.*, config.*,
      manifest.*, billing.*, verdict.*, link pair/renew/revoke,
      session.inject{origin:'peer'}, socket.auth.theft) commit with the bulk
      durability (no fullfsync since S33-10 / P8-D83), then rename the file
      anchor, then AWAIT the keystore write (it starts only after COMMIT
      returned), and only then resolve {anchored: true}; no effect may be
      released before that (a failed keystore write rejects
      ENGINE{anchor-failed}). This is an ordering guarantee against a
      same-user process, not a power-loss guarantee. Close writes durable
      anchors (file AND keystore) carrying the clean-close marker
      `closed: true`; open rewrites them without. BOOT SESSION (boot.ts,
      S33-10): every anchor records `boot` = kern.bootsessionuuid (macOS,
      spawned /usr/sbin/sysctl once per process) / boot_id (Linux) /
      `unknown` (Windows until the parity phase); only the keystore copy is
      authoritative. An anchor ahead of the db is INTEGRITY_BROKEN{truncated}
      with data.cause `tamper` (same boot, clean close, or no comparable
      keystore boot — fails closed) or `unclean-reboot` (boot changed, not a
      clean close); a forged file-anchor boot/closed changes nothing.
      The recovery open (an internal seam, see SEAM HARDENING) opens such a
      log refusing every append and writing no anchor; acknowledgeGap
      ({presenceToken}) (a branded PresenceToken, identity/presence.ts;
      unclean-reboot only) appends one kernel-signed daemon.log.gap{lostFrom, lostTo,
      lostHash, bootPrev, bootNow, fileLag} (contracts kind added, reserved to the
      log) and re-anchors durably; the chain verifies across it. ROUND-2
      HARDENING (research 33 S34-xx): every non-recovery open re-stamps the
      keystore anchor with the current boot and the first append waits for it
      (S34-01; cause semantics = "power loss OR tampering"); non-ahead anchors
      get the full check even in recovery, and a file-only lead with a present
      keystore cannot be acknowledged (FORBIDDEN{unauthenticated-lead},
      S34-04); any lag behind a clean-close KEYSTORE anchor is anchor-lag
      (S34-05); acknowledgeGap leaves recovery only after both anchors succeed
      (S34-12) and rotates a daemon.segment tip first (S34-13). ANCHOR FAULTS
      (S34-02): a post-COMMIT failure on a critical row (file anchor, rotation,
      keystore reject or `keystoreTimeoutMs` timeout, default 5 s, S34-11)
      rejects ENGINE{anchor-failed} and faults the log — bulk continues,
      critical appends re-anchor first or refuse ENGINE{anchor-faulted}; on
      recovery one kernel-signed daemon.anchor.failed{seq, kind} per failed
      row is appended (contracts kind added, reserved); projections must treat
      that seq as not having taken effect. `append` resolves anchored false |
      'both' | 'file-only' (stub keystore), also in anchorStatus.anchoring
      (S34-03); live `integrity` getter (ok | truncated | anchor-faulted);
      close() resolves a CloseReport surfacing a failed keystore write. Verify
      (S34-06): daemon.log.gap / daemon.segment / daemon.anchor.lag /
      daemon.anchor.failed / socket.auth.theft are signature-mandatory, and
      kernel-principal rows must carry a kernel key id (signer + kernelKeyIds)
      — failure code `kernel-key`. Boot ids are validated as UUIDs; sysctl is
      spawned with an empty env (S34-09). ROUND 3: the daemon.anchor.failed
      marker is committed IMMEDIATELY in the post-commit failure path (durable
      fault, S34-17); the fault clears only when every failed row has a marker
      AND both anchors cover a head at/after it (S34-19); close() records
      pending markers before its closing anchors and reports `fault`
      (none|recovered|pending) in CloseReport; on open every critical row
      above the keystore anchor without a marker gets one. An interrupted
      acknowledgeGap (tip = kernel-signed daemon.log.gap naming the keystore
      head as lostTo/lostHash) is re-anchored on open and openForRecovery.
      RESERVED_KINDS exported; verify binds reserved-kind rows to the kernel
      principal + key (`reserved-binding`) and VerifyReport.kernelBinding is
      ok|failed|not-checked (standalone verify without kernel identity) —
      S34-18. Keyless logs are non-rotating and diagnostic-only: the boundary
      append and open-time lag/marker rows refuse before commit with
      FORBIDDEN{kernel-key-required} (S34-22). Boot ids are normalised on both
      sides; `unknown` is not cached and emits onDiagnostic `boot.unknown`
      (S34-21). `fileAnchorMaxMs` test seam (S34-23). Keystore timeout runs
      from dequeue; while a timed-out write is unsettled new writes refuse at
      once (order kept, no hang). KNOWN GAPS — DECIDED: (a) P8-D84 —
      accepted dev-only until T-2026-10-17-009: with the stub keystore (until
      M1.3) every truncation classifies `tamper` and cannot be acknowledged —
      a no-recovery window; recovery path = the quarantine command; (b) P8-D85
      — the S33-07 file-anchor lag > 63 bound is not power-loss-safe
      (un-fsynced renames can roll back), so at open, after the truncation
      check, it is relaxed ONLY when the keystore anchor is present, not
      closed, passes the full anchor check, and records a known boot that
      differs from this known boot, and the file anchor's hash still matches
      its row; a relaxed open appends a kernel-signed
      daemon.anchor.lag{rows, which: 'file', bootPrev, bootNow} (keyless ⇒
      FORBIDDEN{kernel-key-required}); every other case (same boot, unknown
      boot, stub/absent/malformed/pre-D83/closed keystore, closed file anchor;
      pre-D83 ⇒ relax boot-unknown) refuses anchor-lag with `data.relax`
      naming why. The file anchor's own boot is never consulted. A relaxed
      lag defers every open-time anchor write (file rewrite,
      marker/keystore-lag re-anchor) until the signed row is committed
      (evidence first); rotation completion runs first but writes no anchor. S34-24: keystore anchor ahead + any file
      lag ⇒ truncated (cause from the keystore boot); the file anchor still
      gets the hash and clean-close checks, only the > 63 bound is skipped,
      and its lag is TruncationReport.fileLag and daemon.log.gap.fileLag
      (additive). S34-30 CHECKS BEFORE WRITES: on open every integrity check
      (file anchor + lag bounds / relax, keystore anchor + clean-close lag,
      keyless refusals for the open-time marker / keystore-lag rows) runs
      before the first anchor write, rotation completion or row append; a
      crashed rotation is completed with NO anchor writes (the file rewrite
      and the closing keystore re-stamp anchor the head), so a refused open
      leaves both anchors byte-for-byte as found and no queued keystore write
      lands after the throw. OPEN-PATH FOLLOW-UPS (T-2026-10-17-012/013/015):
      S34-31 the keystore anchor is read before the create branch — a home
      with no db but a present/malformed keystore anchor refuses missing-db
      with no file written; the durable file-anchor rewrite runs BEFORE a
      crashed rotation is completed (non-relaxed), so a crash after the seal
      rename reopens as a rotation window; S34-25 a relaxed (P8-D85) open
      whose rotation crashed after the seal rename (lagging file anchor, no
      kernel.db) is accepted iff the same relaxation holds against the sealed
      closing row (pure pre-check, keyless refuses kernel-key-required), the
      segment is created and the relaxed row appended there first, the file
      anchor second; acknowledgeGap completes a rotation with no anchor write
      of its own (the closing row never overwrites the ahead keystore
      anchor). ROTATION FAILURE (T-013(a)): any failure after the active db
      is closed (fsync/fsyncDir EIO, seal rename, next kernel.db) halts the
      instance — closed, timers stopped, append / acknowledgeGap refuse
      ENGINE{rotation-failed} (in-process reason; reads refuse `closed`),
      close() reports keystore 'failed' and writes no anchor; reopen
      completes the rotation. Round 2: the halt starts at the closing-row
      commit (durable file anchor, TRUNCATE checkpoint, journal switch), so
      no row ever follows daemon.segment; a critical append rotates only
      after its anchors settled and resolves anchored even if the rotation
      then halts (surfaced on the next call / close). Stated residual: a
      rotation halt inside acknowledgeGap after the seal rename turns the
      acknowledgeable truncation into missing-db (fail-closed; quarantine). S34-29 (documented): a genesis fsyncDir(home)
      failure leaves kernel.db without a head file ⇒ anchor-missing on every
      later open; manual wipe / quarantine, never auto-recreated.
      `forcedAnchorRate` / `anchorStatus` = status.log telemetry (forced
      anchors per row over the last 10⁴ rows; > 1/64 = outside the measured
      envelope). Open refuses INTEGRITY_BROKEN on missing-db (with anchor,
      sealed segment or keys present), truncated (either anchor ahead of the
      db, with the S33-10 cause), anchor-mismatch, anchor-missing, and anchor-lag (file anchor > 63
      rows behind unless relaxed across a boot change per P8-D85, or any lag
      behind a clean-close anchor); a smaller lag is
      accepted only as a chain prefix and repaired; keystore lag > 1023
      appends a kernel-signed daemon.anchor.lag{rows, which}
      (contracts kind added; bootPrev/bootNow additive for which: 'file'). Fsync
      policy: WAL + synchronous=FULL (default 'process-crash'); 'power-loss'
      adds fullfsync (~10 ms/commit on Apple SSD, above the 5 ms falsifier).
      G-LOG-04 (test:perf, one attempt): bulk p99 < 5 ms / ≤ 0.1 % over 5 ms,
      critical at 1/s under bulk load (60 events in the unit pass) p99 <
      25 ms; the ≥ 600-event 1/s oracle is the manual 10⁶ probe run.
      verify(): full chain (segment links, genesis, anchors) or one segment;
      detects tamper, re-hash forgery, truncation, reorder, stripped or bad
      signatures, unknown keys, relaxed labels. read(): §9.3 projection per
      reader class (human, read-token, community-client, session, mod, peer;
      agent/voice refuse UNSUPPORTED pending M1.7/M4.x). M1.4 SLICE 2 READ
      SIDE: projection uses the EFFECTIVE sensitivity = strictest of the
      registered default, the envelope label and the stored column label (an
      unknown label ranks secret) — labels never widen, and nothing in a
      payload is consulted; readPage({reader, afterSeq, untilSeq?, maxScan,
      kinds?, match?, visit}) re-hashes every envelope (mismatch or
      seq/kind/id column disagreement ⇒ withheld + diagnostic read.withheld),
      returns {lastScanned, scanned, withheld, head, caughtUp}; getById (the
      UNIQUE id index) for ev: refs; onCommit(listener) — tail observers
      called after COMMIT in seq order, exceptions reported as diagnostic
      commit.listener.failed, never reaching the appender; sealed segment
      infos cached (immutable). read() is now built on readPage. ROUND 2:
      releasedHead() = highest seq with no unresolved critical row at or
      below it (a critical row is pending from COMMIT until its anchors
      resolve; failure ⇒ FAILED, withheld forever; failed seqs also loaded
      from daemon.anchor.failed markers); readPage and getById never return
      rows above it; onRelease(listener) wakes tails. readPage projects
      BEFORE hashing for non-human readers, checks prev linkage across
      contiguous rows (prev-mismatch ⇒ withheld) and states the residual (a
      consistent same-user rewrite of a row and every later prev/hash passes
      without signature verification). getById decides visibility before
      the hash (row-integrity only for a row the reader could see).
      Redaction: limited views drop hash/sig/prev, fields → {redacted: true},
      stubs drop the envelope hash; mayBeVisible(e, reader) = metadata-only
      visibility; projectionStats counter. ROUND 3: limited views drop idem
      and refHash too (contracts FIELD_LABELS gain the content-digest audit:
      approval.auto.inputHash, turn.sent.contentHash,
      approval.voice.arm.phrasesHash, approval.voice.intent.handleHash /
      challengeHash, link.msg.out.hash, inject.receipt.shas / source_hash /
      frozenSliceHash); #hashAt uses the sealed-info cache. RESIDUAL until
      M1.3 (header): with the stub keystore, a critical row left unanchored
      by a crash in a previous run is not held out of the released head on
      restart. Not yet: async
      hardware signers, helper anchor, blobs/TTL pruning, projections tables,
      wiring $daemon-lifecycle events into the log.
      Durable-write platform seam (anchor.ts fsyncFile / fsyncDir, the one
      place a path is fsynced; T-2026-10-17-011): on win32 a file is reopened
      'r+' because FlushFileBuffers needs a writable handle (a read-only one is
      EPERM), and the directory fsync is skipped (Windows cannot open a
      directory for flush). STATED RESIDUAL (spec §16, S34-26): on win32 a
      rename is atomic but NOT durable at return — node's renameSync has no
      MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH, so a power cut can undo it; a durable Windows
      rename is parity-phase work (P8-D78). macOS/Linux: the file is reopened
      'r' and fsync(2)ed, the directory fsync(2)ed; an unsupported directory
      fsync (EINVAL/ENOTSUP/EOPNOTSUPP/EISDIR/EBADF, isBenignDirFsyncError) is
      a no-op and any other error (EIO, …) is rethrown (S34-28).
      SEAM HARDENING (T-2026-10-17-009(c), Aegis S34-10, §19.1 release gate):
      `keystoreAnchor` and `bootSession` are no longer KernelLogOptions keys
      (KernelLog.open refuses them at runtime with a TypeError; the private
      constructor demands a module-private construct key held only by open
      and the registered seam closures, so a cast call throws; verifyLog has
      no keystoreAnchor option and always reads the kernel's own store, and
      refuses the key at runtime), and the
      recovery open and acknowledgeGap are no longer public members. The log
      registers them once (class static block) with the internal seam
      channel src/kernel/internal-seams.ts, read only by the internal test
      entry src/kernel/testing.ts (openKernelLog, openKernelLogForRecovery,
      acknowledgeGap, mintTestPresence). test/seams-fence.test.ts proves: no
      src file imports the test entry; only presence.ts, kernel-log.ts,
      manifest/install.ts (M1.5) and testing.ts import the channel; the closure of every tsup entry excludes
      the test entry; no src/cli or $daemon-lifecycle file (nor any other src
      file but the owners) names acknowledgeGap / openForRecovery; dist/
      carries no test-entry marker and its KernelLog / KernelLogOptions types
      expose no seam.
      QUARANTINE (T-2026-10-17-009(a), P8-D89, quarantine.ts + src/cli/log-cli.ts):
      `gnomon log quarantine [--home]` moves the whole home aside intact to the
      sibling <home>.quarantine-<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>-<hex> (Windows-safe name)
      and installs a new home whose log's genesis (seq 1) is the reserved,
      kernel-signed daemon.log.quarantined{quarantinedLog,
      quarantinedHead{seq, hash, segment}, evidencePath, authorizedBy}
      (KernelLog.createQuarantineSuccessor is the only writer; append refuses
      it; verify flags it anywhere but seq 1 and binds it to the kernel key).
      Identity: EVERY head source (db tip, newest sealed segment, file
      anchor, keystore anchor when one exists) is read — dbs from a private
      scratch COPY (kernel.db + -wal, never -shm), so the evidence is never
      written — and the HIGHEST seq is the quarantined head (a truncated db
      behind its anchor is not hidden); all observed heads go in the journal
      and the CLI readout, with a disagreement WARNING before the prompt.
      Crash-safe: daemon lock (acquireDaemonLock) held across steps 1-4 and
      released through the evidence path; build in <evidence>.new, journal,
      rename home→evidence, rename staging→home (ENOENT there = installed by
      another process). completePendingQuarantine (run by every quarantine,
      by the CLI first and by startDaemon before it creates a home) installs
      an interrupted quarantine ONLY if the unsigned journal checks out:
      staging = evidence + '.new', evidence a quarantine-named sibling, both
      plain dirs owned by this uid (no symlink/junction), the evidence holds
      the journal's quarantinedLog, and the staging log's row 1 is a verified
      daemon.log.quarantined matching newLog / evidencePath / quarantinedLog;
      anything else is abandoned (reported, never deleted). These checks stop
      accidents and planted junk; they are NOT authenticity (the journal is
      unsigned and the staging keys are its own) — that waits for M1.3. The
      staging genesis is checked on rows/chain/signature only, never via
      verifyLog (which reads the per-user keystore anchor; M1.3 tripwire test
      → T-2026-10-18-007), and the keystore anchor is consulted only for the
      default home (spec §22 OPEN 19). Unreadable dbs are reported
      (`unreadable: copy failed: CODE`), ENOSPC refuses no-space; EPERM/EBUSY
      on the install refuses install-busy (retry; the pending install
      finishes). A crash between the step-4 rename and the lock release
      leaves our pidfile in the evidence, reported as lockResidue. An interrupted
      install is reported as outcome completed-pending (CLI: COMPLETED).
      Refuses daemon-running, not-a-home, nothing-to-quarantine,
      quarantine-pending, home-busy (EPERM/EBUSY/EACCES on the move),
      kernel-key-required. The genesis principal comes from kernelPrincipalOf
      (the daemon's root-authority source). CLI output: src/cli/readout.ts
      (#gnomon-kernel; instrument-readout voice — readout rows, `REFUSED  CODE
      reason`, indented `next:`); canConnectSocket shared in
      lifecycle/endpoint.ts. RESIDUALS: authorizedBy 'interactive-confirm' is
      NO PROOF OF PRESENCE — an interactive TTY + typed last 8 chars of the
      log id, passable by any same-user process owning a terminal, until
      presence lands at M1.3; at M1.3 the keystore anchor item must move with
      the evidence; a stale pidfile of a dead daemon is DELETED from the
      evidence by the lock acquisition, its bytes kept in the journal
      (stalePidfile, base64) — the only change to the old home.
      `npm run
      test:perf` = scripts/test-perf.mjs (cmd.exe-safe; --passWithNoTests on
      win32 only, a missing perf file fails loudly elsewhere).
    path: src/kernel/log/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, log, paradigm-8, pr-4, m1.2, p8-d82, p8-d83, p8-d84, p8-d85]
    aspects: ["~ample-internal-logs"]
    tests:
      - test/kernel-log.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-posix.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-gap.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-crossboot.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-open-order.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-rotation-fault.test.ts

      - test/boot-session.test.ts
      - test/durable-fs.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-quarantine.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-quarantine-names.test.ts
      - test/log-cli.test.ts
      - test/log-cli-quarantine-posix.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOG-01, G-LOG-02, G-LOG-03, G-LOG-04, G-LOG-06, G-LOG-07, G-LOG-08, G-LOG-09, G-LOG-10, G-LOG-11]

  gnomon-ts-client:
    description: >-
      #gnomon-ts-client (spec §4.9, §5, §12.8; §19.1 M1.12 slice 2).
      src/clients/ts/ — the published TypeScript client, built ONLY on
      @a-company/gnomon-contracts + node:* (fenced by test/m112-conformance):
      hello with serverSig verified against the PINNED kernel key before any
      credential is sent; device-key auth (gnomon:auth:v1) or read-token proof
      of possession (gnomon:auth-token:v1); call() typed for the 12 verbs + 2
      relays (ClientMethodParams/Results); refusals reject with RefusedError;
      subscribe() = log.read{follow} with rows in seq order and a visible end;
      logging through an injected ClientLogPort (Paradigm-logger shaped).
      Re-exports the one §4.5 sanitizer for UIs.
    tags: [gnomon, m1.12, client]
    oracles: [G-CLI-02]

  client-conformance:
    description: >-
      #client-conformance (spec §12.8, G-CLI-02; M1.12 slice 2).
      src/clients/conformance/ — driver.ts: the language-neutral JSON-lines
      driver protocol (connect/call/subscribe/events/closed/close/sanitize/
      row/denominator), TsClientDriver (in-process, JSON round-tripped) and
      SubprocessDriver (any language over stdio); runner.ts: runs every
      CONFORMANCE_CHECKS `run` check against a live kernel, `slice-2` checks
      reported todo, report + conformanceReportHash; hello.server-sig-mismatch
      runs an impostor endpoint that copies the real hello's fields with a
      FORGED (random) serverSig (zero auth frames) — not a replay of a
      genuinely signed hello, which is the open T-2026-10-18-016 gap; the
      impostor listens on a private socket file (POSIX) or a random named
      pipe (Windows) and a listen error fails the check instead of crashing
      the runner. auth.device-refused also requires that the client keeps no
      live connection after the AUTH refusal. subscribe.since checks the
      backlog against a non-follow page (since+1, no gaps); contiguity
      assumes the fixture's full-visibility principal, a restricted principal
      must use the oracle-page comparison only. Falsifiers (T-2026-10-18-015):
      a client that checks the pin's PEM hash and keyId but never serverSig
      fails only hello.server-sig-mismatch, at case (c); a client that keeps a
      connection after the refusal fails auth.device-refused. status.clients[] copies only
      {suite, version, reportHash} (sanitized, capped) and labels a
      declared tuple `declared` and reports clientConformance{n, of} (G-OBS-01).
    tags: [gnomon, m1.12, client, conformance]
    oracles: [G-CLI-02, G-OBS-01, G-HON-03]

  approval-broker:
    description: >-
      #approval-broker (spec §3.4, §5 verb 8, §3.1 approvals key; §19.1 M1.6).
      src/kernel/broker/. classes.ts — valid decisions per card kind and the
      BASE presence class of each (cached: allow/deny on permission, answer,
      approve-plan, reject on plan/reconsider/inbound handoff, deny on
      strictness, incident; fresh: everything else the spec names, incl.
      always; root: install, use-api-token, peer-pair, grant; none: withdraw);
      irreversible and peer flags raise a cached decision to fresh; a decision
      the spec does not class is fresh (fail closed). card.ts — the card is
      composed from the broker's own record: lines sanitized (hidden-chars /
      bidi → visible escapes + card flags, §4.5), contentHash over {kind,
      requestedBy, hsid, detail, flags}, actions[] kernel-signed per valid
      decision (domain gnomon:card-action:v1), and the presence prompt composed
      from the card record (kind, requester, contentHash summary, connId, open
      GUI sessions {n}, the class reason) and signed by the kernel key (domain
      gnomon:presence-prompt:v1); verifyPresencePrompt checks text = the
      record's composition. Line cap MAX_LINE (512 code points, exported for
      #manifest-loader's install-card wrapping); the presence summary takes
      the first line's first 120 code points, never splitting a surrogate
      pair. install-card.ts passes the install card's pre-sanitized lines
      together with their sanitizer flags. unlock.ts — per-connection cached unlock keyed by
      connId, created only by a fresh presence on that connection, never for a
      CLI connection, dropped on close (approval.unlock.dropped{reason:closed})
      or after 15 min idle (reason idle), never usable from another
      connection. broker.ts — ApprovalBroker: raise (approval.request before
      the card exists; permission cards time out into deny, fallback and
      api-token never expire), approve (human only, B4; voice FORBIDDEN gate
      voice `voice-off` for every form, G-ABI-02 voice row; via link refused
      `peer-via`; held ⇒ withdraw only; peer-flagged always refused
      `peer-always`; decisionSig by the approvals key over approveMessage —
      bad ⇒ AUTH bad-signature, no approvals key ⇒ UNSUPPORTED presence
      M1.3; presence per class with a PresenceToken BOUND to {cardId,
      contentHash, decision} on the kernel-trusted clock (T-2026-10-18-003);
      always under a cached unlock ⇒ FORBIDDEN presence when no fresh presence
      comes; root class signs the decision with the root key; approval.decide
      {decisionSig, presence, via, connId, cli provenance} BEFORE the effect;
      always ⇒ approval.rule.learned; approval.suspect{why, provenance} for
      unknown CLI provenance / no TTY / CLI pgid inside a live session /
      unlock beyond class), expireDue, decisionOf (silence and unknown cards
      ⇒ deny, B6), connectionClosed, status. Production presence is
      PRESENCE_UNAVAILABLE, the approvals key NO_APPROVALS_KEY and the root
      unprovisioned until M1.3: every presence-taking decision refuses.
      install-card.ts — the M1.5 install-card decision moved under the broker
      (T-2026-10-18-008): the card is rendered from the detail RE-DERIVED from
      the pinned snapshot (manifest/install.ts rederiveInstallDetail) and kept
      in kernel memory; presence is a mod.install token bound to {cardId,
      contentHash} handed unburned to decideInstall. api-token.ts — the per-use
      billing api-token card (human-only request, root+fresh use-api-token,
      never expires), keystore injection into exactly one child env
      (takeEnvFor, single use; KEYSTORE_UNAVAILABLE until M1.3),
      billing.api.card.requested / active (activation and every turn) /
      ended / refused and the status indicator API CREDITS ACTIVE. port.ts —
      the #socket port: approve, permit.reserve (+ release), connection close,
      status cards / budget / billing, guiSessions[].unlock. The spec declares
      no signals; events are §9.1 approval.* / billing.* kinds. Round 2
      (Aegis C1): GUI vs CLI is decided by the authenticated credential (only
      a launch-token GUI session may hold an unlock), never by the hello
      surface; expiry skips a card being decided and the decision re-checks
      the card and the connection after its last await (one terminal row);
      the sanitizer strips tag characters and escapes C0/C1 controls; the
      install card is rendered by the broker from re-derived lines, readPaths
      and linked source validated, presence bound to the decision; effect
      failures are recorded approval.suspect{why:effect-failed}
      (approval.decide is a record, never authority).
    path: src/kernel/broker/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, broker, approvals, presence, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    tests:
      - test/approval-broker.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/manifest-loader.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-01, G-BRK-02, G-BRK-03, G-BRK-05, G-ABI-02, G-SUB-02, G-SUB-03]

  budget-fuse:
    description: >-
      #budget-fuse as a METER (spec §3.4, §4.8, §10 laws 1/7; §19.1 M1.6;
      founder ruling P8-D92 "track everything, enforce nothing"). Only law 7
      is enforced: permit.reserve of every kind but tool.permission (Aegis L1) on claude-code runs the
      billing scan (env, settings env blocks, apiKeyHelper, mcp.json env
      blocks, any *_BASE_URL incl. loopback; unreadable layers are hits) and
      refuses BILLING_REFUSED, unless the kernel's own spawn of the card's
      hsid holds the per-use api-token (scan still run, only the injected key
      ignored). Every other reserve is granted with a kernel-signed permit:v1
      (attribution, law 1). Removed by P8-D92: day caps, compute and
      concurrency caps, the window threshold and 30 % human reserve, the
      breaker pause, the armada pause and budget card, the resume allowance.
      Tracked per UTC day: tokens + USD per user (P8-D91: an agent's spend
      in its human's bucket; before M1.7 attributed to the root's human
      principal, recorded attributed: root-human on permit.reserve), repo,
      loop, session, model, backend; local-LLM sessions with model and
      projects; subscription-window usage; quota errors (budget.window
      {quotaError}); ladder rungs 50/75/90/100 % as informational
      budget.ladder rows; over-permit usage recorded once (budget.usage
      {overPermit}). Usage only via recordStreamUsage(turn.usage|turn.result);
      malformed ⇒ budget.usage{malformed} and dropped. status.budget exposes
      the spend breakdown (byUser/byRepo/byLoop/bySession/byModel/byBackend,
      local, subscriptionWindow, quotaErrors). config.ts: root-signed
      tracking/display keys only (reference amounts, window shape) with a
      version high-water mark (sidecar ⊔ last signed config.loaded on the
      chain); unsigned/invalid/older/missing-after-signed ⇒ defaults + a
      logged row; project.yaml fuse/budget/strictness/review keys ignored and
      logged. parseReserveParams: no usage params; agents never name
      repo/loop/session/machine (kernel-bound, M1.7). DEFERRED: counters in
      memory (boot rebuild beside S-L2). M1.7 note: api-token session turn
      permits are labelled subscription; per-turn billing.api.active needs
      usage against the spawn permit (G-SUB-03).
    path: src/kernel/budget/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, budget, fuse, law-7, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    tests:
      - test/budget-fuse.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LAW-01, G-LAW-01b, G-BUD-01, G-BUD-02, G-BUD-03, G-SUB-04]

  scheduler:
    description: >-
      #scheduler (spec §3.5, §2.4 rule 3, §9.1 schedule.*, §10 law 5; M1.9) in
      src/kernel/scheduler/. Log-derived timers (timer:v1, never stored): the
      last fired due instant of a key is the max dueAt of its
      schedule.automation.due rows, so start() rebuilds every timer from the
      records + the log and no instant fires twice (G-SCH-01). automation:v1
      {id, trigger: {at} | {every, anchor}, model: local|claude|any, attended}
      is a LOCAL shape (not a wire object yet; moving it into
      gnomon-contracts would move clientApiId). Every automation is attended:
      attended:false is REFUSED (schedule.dropped{reason:
      'unattended-unavailable'}) until an #approval-broker decision (M1.6) and
      the run step (M1.7/M1.8) exist (law 5). Instants are strict ISO-8601
      with Z or ±hh:mm; every is 1 min..10 years. Rebuild trusts only
      kernel-principal due rows whose dueAt is not after their own at
      (kernel principals = the unprovisioned p:00000000/kernel and the
      current one, so root provisioning at M1.3 does not refire timers). One daemon ⇒ one timer per key: duplicates
      (N worktrees) are dropped {reason:'duplicate-key'} (G-LAW-05b). An
      instant due while down — or older than one period capped at the
      horizon (a slept host) — fires as an attended event (late:true);
      several missed instants coalesce (schedule.dropped{reason:'coalesced',
      count}). A due row spends nothing; the run step is hard-wired to refuse
      ENGINE{not-implemented} (session.refused) — no spawn, no permit
      (G-LAW-05). Wakes are capped at 60 s; a failed append releases the hold
      and retries in 5 s; stop() is a latch returning the in-flight tick,
      which the daemon awaits before stopping children; the scheduler starts
      before the idle monitor. While the next due instant is inside 10 min
      the scheduler holds idle exit ('timer' holder). Clock and wake are seams
      (now, arm), so the 24 h soak is a unit test. Daemon wiring: DaemonOptions
      .automations (none by default; root-signed config later), handle.scheduler.
    path: src/kernel/scheduler/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, scheduler, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/scheduler.test.ts
      - test/scheduler-daemon.test.ts
    oracles: [G-SCH-01, G-LAW-05, G-LAW-05b]

  session-runner:
    description: >-
      #session-runner (spec §3.3 Preflight/Environment, §3.4 law 7, §16;
      M1.7 slice 1) in src/kernel/runner/. preflight.ts: the claude binary by
      explicit path (root-signed config) else the platform registry's known
      locations (src/platform/claude-code.ts), NEVER PATH (p01); realpath +
      sha256; claude_binary_changed on a changed sha; version >= 2.1.288;
      `auth status` = claude.ai/firstParty. law7Layers: EVERY layer the child
      reads for THIS spawn's cwd (T-009 / Aegis C5) — managed sources per OS
      (+ managed-settings.d/*.json; unscannable sources such as Windows policy
      keys are hits), user settings*.json, project + worktree
      .claude/settings(.local).json and .mcp.json, ~/.claude.json mcpServers
      AND projects.<cwd>.mcpServers, the session's own policy.json/mcp.json —
      scanned over the CHILD env (scanApiBilling). env.ts: allowlist + scrub +
      GNOMON_HSID/SOCK/SESSION_KEY, envKeys names only (G-RUN-02). The
      darwin `session` profile generator is src/platform/darwin/session-sbpl.ts
      (from the M1.5 SessionPolicy + session-denies registry; T-008 git
      denies: .git pointer file, config.worktree, modules/**/{hooks,config,
      info}; launchers open/osascript/lsregister/launchctl by realpath),
      registered in src/kernel/sandbox/session.ts (no backend => refused
      sandbox-unavailable; status fidelity per platform, G-WIN-06).
      p10m-claude-compat-generated (probes/) re-ran real Claude Code 2.1.302
      under the GENERATED profile with the adapter argv: PASS, with writes to
      the whole ~/.claude/** and ~/.claude.json denied and NO per-session
      CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (a custom CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR loses subscription auth).
      ROUND 2 (Aegis H1/M1/M2/L2): binary.ts pins the claude sha256 in
      <home>/claude-pin.json on first use (logged session.sandbox{phase:
      'binary-pin'}); another hash is claude_binary_changed until an explicit
      repinClaudeBinary (CLI/test seam until the gate-class card); the kernel
      executes only a private copy <home>/bin/claude-<sha> written from the
      same verified buffer, and preflight --version / auth status run that
      copy UNDER the session profile. The profile also write-denies the
      binary realpath, its versions dir and every known-location link
      (supervisor binaryDenies) and the user persistence paths
      (policy.ts PERSISTENCE_PATHS: LaunchAgents, shell rc files, git config,
      ~/.ssh); shortcuts/automator join the launcher exec denies; copied
      Apple launchers die by launch constraints (137, spec §A.2(iv)). Law 7
      also flags ~/.claude.json primaryApiKey / customApiKeyResponses.approved
      and forceLoginMethod 'console' (billing.ts). The p10m re-run with the
      private copy and the new denies: PASS.
      ROUND 3: trust on first use — the pin is written only after the
      sandboxed preflight passes (N1); the copy (not the source) is hashed,
      streamed, and not re-hashed when size+mtime match the pin record;
      script installs (#!, the npm cli.js) are refused
      claude_binary_unsupported; `gnomon session repin-claude`
      (src/cli/session-cli.ts: TTY + typed last 8 of the new sha, daemon
      stopped) writes a re-pin the supervisor logs session.sandbox{binary-pin,
      by:'repin', from, to} at the next open; PERSISTENCE_PATHS adds
      .bash_login, .zlogout, ~/.config/fish.
      macOS FLOOR (P8 note, CI run 39100281741): SESSION_SANDBOX_MIN_MACOS = 15
      (platform/darwin/session-sbpl.ts, read via platform/darwin/macos.ts
      sw_vers); below it the darwin session backend refuses
      sandbox-unavailable {floor:macos, have, need} and status shows the
      session sandbox unavailable — on macos-14 a copied osascript reached
      System Events from inside the profile. CI tests macos-15 (enforcement)
      and macos-14 (floor refusal).
      SIP FLOOR (CI run 39100811385, GitHub macos-15 runner, SIP off: a copied
      launchctl not SIGKILLed): darwinSipEnabled (platform/darwin/macos.ts,
      csrutil status, cached; Custom Configuration counts as disabled) — SIP
      not plainly enabled ⇒ sandbox-unavailable {floor:sip, have:disabled|
      unknown}, status unavailable reason sip-disabled; test seam
      SupervisorOptions.sipEnabled / sessionLaunchPlan's 4th arg. Profile v2
      adds (deny mach-lookup (global-name "com.apple.coreservices.appleevents"))
      beside (deny appleevent-send) (APPLEEVENT_MACH_SERVICES); Claude verified
      working (p10m-claude-compat-generated pass). Its coverage of a non-Apple
      AppleEvent sender is UNPROVEN (spec OPEN 5): the existing copied-osascript
      check (`get name` of System Events) is answered without sending an event,
      so its exit is only RECORDED (test annotation), never asserted; the 137
      launch-constraint kill is asserted on SIP-on macOS 15+ only.
      Aegis conditions: darwin automation:apple-events fidelity is 'partial'
      while spec OPEN 5 is open (status must not overclaim); inherited TCC
      grants of the responsible process + the reachable pasteboard are spec
      §A.2(v) / OPEN 22 (recommend launching gnomond by launchd or the signed
      app); the csrutil parser test keeps the exact macOS 15 stdout fixture.
    path: src/kernel/runner/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, session, law-7, paradigm-8, m1.7]
    tests:
      - test/session-preflight.test.ts
      - test/session-profile.test.ts
      - test/session-sandbox.test.ts
    oracles: [G-SUB-01, G-RUN-02, G-RUN-06, G-SBX-04, G-SBX-05, G-SBX-06, G-SBX-08, G-WIN-06]

  supervisor:
    description: >-
      #supervisor (spec §3.3, §6.1, §5 verbs 2-4; M1.7 slice 1) in
      src/kernel/supervisor/. The one place `claude -p` starts. Slice 1:
      ATTENDED HUMAN-started Claude Code sessions only (others FORBIDDEN;
      the scheduler run step still refuses). open: session.open -> preflight
      -> session dir policy.json/mcp.json from generated plugin bytes
      VERIFIED against the kernel-held record (T-008, manifest/generate.ts
      verifiedGenerated) -> per-spawn law-7 scan (hit => billing.api.refused +
      session.refused{api_billing_refused}, BILLING_REFUSED, before any
      permit) -> permit.reserve{session.spawn, claude-code, kernelSpawn} ->
      assertClaudePermit (Aegis: never claude without a live law-7-scanned
      claude-code session.spawn permit for this hsid) -> generated session
      profile -> DaemonChildren.intend (intent row BEFORE fork) -> spawn
      detached -> track (boot + start-time witness) -> session.sandbox{hashes}.
      stdout: every line a turn.event first (S2); usage only from stream
      events against the SPAWN permit (fuse.recordStreamUsage); ask-user /
      plan cards via the broker; init.apiKeySource != none kills the group;
      quota recorded (P8-D92 tracking-only fuse). send: a turn permit (caller's
      validated or kernel-reserved), law 7 re-scanned, session.inject{client}
      -> turn.sent -> stream-json user line. stop: interrupt = control_request
      only; else staged (control message, stdin close, DaemonChildren.stop
      SIGTERM -> SIGKILL on the group). Mid-session watch (G-SUB-05):
      layersHash + gitHash polled every 500 ms; a law-7 hit stops the session
      within 2 s. port.ts: the dispatcher's session port (human class only);
      daemon.ts wires it. ROUND 2: the exit listener is attached synchronously
      at spawn (an early-exiting child is cleaned up: watch cleared, permits
      released, moved to a bounded history); one catch from reserve to track
      releases the permit, kills a stray child, removes the session dir;
      layers hashed before the law-7 scan and re-checked right before spawn;
      child HOME forced to the scanned user home; caller turn permits must be
      live, unexpired, subscription and single-use; turn permits FIFO; the
      watch hashes ~/.claude.json only through its law-7 projection;
      stdout paused above a queue high-water mark and lines over 64 KiB
      logged as {lineHash, bytes}; init without apiKeySource logged absent.
      ROUND 3: 'exit' only resumes paused stdout; the release path runs on
      'close' so a result written just before exit is recorded; #wire runs
      before the post-track log row, and a failure after track stops the
      group (the exit path releases the permits); a stray child before track
      is SIGKILLed as a group; preflight spawns are async; used turn-permit
      ids are pruned on expiry; status shows the claude pin.
    path: src/kernel/supervisor/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, session, paradigm-8, m1.7]
    tests:
      - test/session-supervisor.test.ts
    oracles: [G-RUN-03, G-SUB-05]

  local-runner:
    description: >-
      #local-runner (spec §13, §3.3, §6.1 step 2; M1.8 slice 1). Kernel side:
      src/kernel/local/endpoint.ts (loopback/LAN rule on the normalised host;
      hostnames, Anthropic hosts, link-local/metadata, public, userinfo,
      forbidden web-endpoint port refused — G-SUB-06 BILLING_REFUSED via
      config.ts), config.ts (compat names = gnomon-local allowlist, no cloud;
      assertNotClaude: law 7 never runs claude under a backend:local permit),
      probe.ts (/slots n_ctx, else /props; tools only on a live tool call),
      routing.ts (local|claude|any; capable = probe ok + tools (or text-tool
      opt-in) + n_ctx >= floor 8192), src/adapters/local/runner.ts (pi rpc
      JSONL driver, tolerant parse, LocalUsageMeter no double count),
      src/kernel/supervisor/local-session.ts (LocalLane: incapable local =>
      fallback card via the broker + model.fallback.requested +
      MODEL_UNAVAILABLE{cardId}, no spawn; fallback-cloud decision opens
      Claude, S7; any => session.routed; capable => local session.spawn
      permit recorded with model + repo (P8-D92 meter, never capped) ->
      session profile -> intend/track kind local-runner; stdout turn.event
      first, model.capabilities/model.repair, usage against the spawn
      permit). Round 2: tools confined to realpath roots (no .git, no symlink final component), probe bodies capped 1 MiB, configured model id preferred, assertNotClaude on realpaths + @anthropic-ai/claude-code, explicit backend pins the route, launchPlan TEST-RIG seam (test/local-lane-spawn.test.ts). Slice 2: darwin e2e rig (G-LCL-01 fake half, G-LCL-06, 6-child
      G-LAW-01c), root-signed local.yaml, gnomon probe local, compaction,
      daemon config wiring, attended local automations.
    path: src/kernel/local/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, local, paradigm-8, m1.8]
    tests:
      - test/local-runner.test.ts
    oracles: [G-SUB-06, G-LCL-02, G-LCL-03, G-ADP-02, G-LAW-01c]

  claude-adapter:
    description: >-
      #claude-adapter (spec §2.2, §3.3; M1.7 slice 1) in
      src/adapters/claude-code/adapter.ts: the §3.3 argv with FORBIDDEN_FLAGS
      throwing (G-RUN-01, also --flag=value and extras); stream-json parse,
      TOLERANT (unknown fields ignored, unknown types `unknown`, non-objects
      `malformed` — the falsifier); UsageMeter charges each message id once
      and the result only its remainder; 4 MiB line cap; the stdin user and
      control_request interrupt lines. fixtures/2.1.302-turn.jsonl replays
      the shapes p10m-claude-compat-generated observed (G-ADP-01, synthetic;
      recorded nightly replay is slice 2).
    path: src/adapters/claude-code/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, adapter, stream-json, paradigm-8, m1.7]
    tests:
      - test/claude-adapter.test.ts
    oracles: [G-RUN-01, G-ADP-01]

  mod-host:
    description: >-
      #mod-host (spec §3.8, §12.1-§12.3, §15.5 row mod; §19.1 M1.11 slice 1)
      in src/kernel/modhost/. host.ts ModHost - one child per mod, started
      ONLY via #manifest-loader loadMod (root-signed grant.install, snapshot
      integrity, manifestHash, grants; a changed linked folder pauses and
      stages the re-approve card instead of loading, G-MOD-08); effects in
      force = union of tool effects, capped at read while grant.install{dev}
      is live, an expired dev period pauses (mod.dev.expired, fail closed);
      spawns node <index.mjs|index.js> from the pinned snapshot under the
      default-deny mod profile (sandbox/mod.ts registry +
      platform/darwin/mod-sbpl.ts; same floor as sessions: macOS 15+ and SIP;
      other platforms refuse sandbox-unavailable), env GNOMON_MOD_NAME +
      GNOMON_EGRESS_PROXY only; tracked by DaemonChildren kind mod IN MEMORY
      (no mod.spawned/mod.exited kind: adding one moves clientApiId);
      init must echo mod-abi:v1 and MOD_ABI_ID. channel.ts - JSON-RPC 2.0
      NDJSON over stdio (1 MiB frames, stderr tail). Calls serialized per mod
      so mod->host requests are attributed exactly; effects.ts enforces
      state.get=read, state.set=write, kernel.call by verb (approve never,
      log.append only own declared modns, session.send only inside a tool
      call with the caller hsid) => FORBIDDEN + mod.effect.refused. Timeout =>
      cancel{cancelToken}, unhonoured => restart; crash => mod.health{ok:false}
      + restart (cap 3/10 min). block.ts closed kit:v1 gate (256 KiB / 2000
      nodes, sanitizeLine, Button command id only, Image alt + sha256 blob,
      Canvas names a ds). relayView/relayCommand stamp relayedFor from the
      caller. egress-proxy.ts per-mod loopback CONNECT proxy (443 only;
      granted declared hosts; IP literals, private names, Anthropic hosts,
      private resolutions and calls lacking network refused). state.ts
      per-mod KV <home>/mods/<name>/state.db (sibling of state/, never granted; O_NOFOLLOW reads, random O_EXCL tmp; JSON in slice 1). Round 2 (Aegis): state/ and blobs/ must be real dirs at their expected paths before every profile build; tunnels closed at call end; proxy head deadline, connection cap, idle timeout, resolve-check-connect, wider non-public ranges; explicit verb-effect table; session.send pinned to the caller hsid, permitId stripped; channel caps in-flight mod requests at 16 with backpressure.
      Test seams (unsandboxed launcher, egress dialer) only via
      kernel/testing.ts createModHostForTest. SLICE 2: call-id binding
      (host mints callId on view.render/command.run/tool.call, the SDK echoes
      it on state.get/state.set/kernel.call; a foreign or ended callId is
      FORBIDDEN modCode CALL + mod.effect.refused{why:call-id}; none = idle;
      modAbiId moved 00ae702b -> 7aaf92f4, clientApiId unchanged);
      bridge.ts createModKernelCall (log.append own modns clamped to
      kind/payload<=16 KiB/audience/sensitivity, Aegis C2, appended under the
      mod principal; every other verb via dispatcher.callAs as a
      connection-less mod principal) and createModRelayPort (mod.view /
      mod.command start the mod on first use; relayedFor stamped by the
      dispatcher from the caller connection; GUI mod.command refused until a
      gesture proof, M1.10); startDaemon constructs the host, status.mods +
      status.modAbiId, stopAll before the children sweep at every exit;
      folder watch on a running linked mod (fs.watch recursive, .git
      ignored, 500 ms debounce => stop{changed} + loadMod pause + re-approve
      card). Round 2 (review + Aegis): own-modns appends go as:'userland'
      under the mod principal, kernel-key-signed as an attestation
      (attributed by principal, never keyId; spec §3.2 L7); relay admission
      ModHost.admit (name/id shapes refused BAD_REQUEST in dispatch; an
      uninstalled name NOT_INSTALLED with manifest.refuse folded past 8 per
      10 s; paused answered from memory; relay starts count against
      RESTART_CAP; DOWN until an operator start); per-mod append budget (20
      rows/s, 64 KiB per call) and 8 mod->host requests in flight per mod;
      status.mods full to the human class only, counts otherwise; watcher
      unarmed on exit/timeout/pause/cap and re-armed on a successful start;
      a lost mod.paused / mod.effect.refused row surfaces as
      daemon.mod.row.unrecorded. Round 3 (Aegis R2-1/R2-2): fold.ts RowFold -
      mod.effect.refused (per mod, keyed effect|why|verb, verb clipped) and
      relay-miss manifest.refuse rows: 8 individual per 10 s window, the rest
      one coalesced row on the timer / next window / stop / stopAll, at most
      16 keys + other<Field>s + distinct (exact to 1024, then distinctCapped);
      the bridge drops a call's byte tally on the host's onCallEnd and prunes
      past idle seconds (never a wholesale clear); kernel-side refusals reach
      a mod as code + reason only (generic message, no wrapped data).
      Slice 3: spend from the caller permit, dev-mode cards/re-pin,
      gnomon-mods MCP bridge, gnomon mod CLI, /blob (C4), Tier 0, workshop.
    tags: [kernel, mods, sandbox, M1.11]
flows:
  daemon-lifecycle:
    description: >-
      $daemon-lifecycle (spec §2.4; PR-5 core of M1.9). (1) client activation —
      `gnomon daemon --detach` re-execs gnomond with the clean env and home in
      argv, then waits for the socket; (2) one per user — home 0700 read back,
      endpoint path chosen (never /tmp), O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile acquired
      (live holder refuses with daemon.lock.refused, dead holder reclaimed);
      (3) #kernel-log opened with the kernel key (M1.4 seam: a supplied or
      ephemeral in-memory software Ed25519 key, custody partial; keystore
      item is M1.3) and the root authority (default unprovisioned), #socket
      listens at 0600 with the hello/auth dispatcher, daemon.start is
      emitted AND logged (daemon.exit logged at shutdown, then the log is
      closed before the lock is released); (4) idle exit —
      no open connections for idleMs (default 10 min) ⇒ daemon.exit{reason:
      'idle'}, socket closed, pidfile and socket released only if still ours;
      SIGTERM/SIGINT take the same path with reason 'signal'. Since M1.4
      slice 2 every log.read{follow} subscription is an idle holder
      ('subscriber') and the hub is closed first at shutdown. Not yet built:
      sessions/timers as holders, mod stop, orphan sweep, the Windows pipe.
      M1.4: the #web-endpoint is started by requestWebLaunch, its WebSocket
      connections are idle holders, and it is stopped (child ended) before
      the socket closes at shutdown. M1.9: (5) home — the daemon takes its
      home only from --home (daemonHomeOf; GNOMON_HOME is a client-side test
      seam it never reads — both gnomond paths, foreground and detaching;
      only `gnomon` client commands honour the seam); (6) orphan sweep at
      start, before the socket listens — every KERNEL-written session.spawned
      with no session.exited/orphaned is recorded session.orphaned{probe,
      action} BEFORE any signal, and its group is signalled only when the
      row's boot session (log/boot.ts) equals this boot AND the live
      leader's start-time witness (process-control.ts: macOS ps lstart,
      Linux /proc stat field 22, read with TZ=UTC before any await at track;
      ps forks — never on a hot path; win32 = parity phase, never signalled)
      equals the recorded one — else probe stale-boot | unattributable,
      action none; orphans stopped in parallel, counted only when gone
      (G-DMN-04); (7) supervised children (DaemonChildren: session,
      local-runner, mod) — intend() writes session.spawned{phase:'intent'}
      before the fork, track() writes {phase:'running', pid, pgid, boot,
      startTime} and refuses a session child that does not lead its own
      group; sessions write session.exited and hold idle ('session'); mods
      never hold, and have no spawn/exit kinds in §9.1, so a crash-orphaned
      mod is not swept (residual); stop = SIGTERM → grace → SIGKILL, then
      group members outliving the leader are SIGKILLed and counted;
      every shutdown (idle included) stops the scheduler, then stops and
      AWAITS every child (mod.stopped{reason}) and the web transport child
      (socket.web.stop{reason:'idle'|'daemon-exit'}) BEFORE daemon.exit
      {reason, liveChildren} is written — the falsifier "daemon exits with a
      live child"; (8) idle holders are connections, subscribers (log.read
      follow AND open GUI WebSockets), sessions and #scheduler timers due
      inside 10 min. Not yet built: the session runner and mod host that
      call children.track (M1.7/M1.8/M1.11), the Windows pipe, job object
      and owner-only DACL read-back (G-WIN-02, parity phase P8-D78).
    steps:
      - "#gnomon-kernel"
      - "#kernel-log"
      - "#identity"
      - "#socket"
      - "#scheduler"
      - "#web-endpoint"
    tests:
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/home-resolution.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
      - test/daemon-children.test.ts
      - test/scheduler-daemon.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02, G-DMN-03, G-DMN-04, G-DMN-05]

gates:
  subscription-only:
    description: >-
      ^subscription-only (spec §10 law 7, D35): no kernel-spawned Claude
      process bills API credits — #budget-fuse refuses permit.reserve
      {session.spawn|turn, claude-code} BILLING_REFUSED on any API-billing
      path the law-7 scan finds; the only API path is the per-use root-signed
      api-token card of #approval-broker, injected into exactly one child,
      with the API CREDITS ACTIVE indicator and billing.api.active rows.
      M1.7 slice 1: #session-runner scans every layer the child reads per
      spawn cwd before any permit, #supervisor refuses claude without a
      law-7-scanned claude-code spawn permit and watches the layers
      mid-session (G-SUB-01, G-SUB-05).
    tags: [gnomon, law-7, billing, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    oracles: [G-SUB-02, G-SUB-03, G-SUB-04]

aspects:
  ~ample-internal-logs:
    description: >-
      ~ample-internal-logs (spec §9) — everything the kernel does is an event:v1
      row in #kernel-log with the §9.1 default labels set by the appender
      (audience private; turn.event bodies, session.inject bytes,
      approval.request.tool_input, oracle evidence and voice text sensitive;
      a kind without a default is refused, G-LOG-09), and every read goes
      through the §9.3 redaction matrix (stubs {kind, hsid, at, hash} for
      redacting readers, secret never to a read token, nothing to a peer).
      PR-4 covers the label and read-projection halves; retention/TTL
      pruning (§9.4), status metrics (§9.6) and the honesty CI (§9.7) follow.
    category: observability
    severity: high
    tags: [gnomon, logging, redaction, paradigm-8]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/log/chain.ts
      - src/kernel/log/redaction.ts
    applies-to:
      - "#kernel-log"
